The user wants a translation of Go code to PHP.
The Go code reads n and m, computes a matrix exponentiation modulo 1e9+7, and prints the result.
Specific logic:
- If n < m, print 1.
- If n == m, print 2.
- Else construct a companion matrix of size m x m.
  - matrix[0][0] = 1, matrix[0][m-1] = 1.
  - matrix[i][i-1] = 1 for i=1..m-1.
- Compute matrix^n.
- Print element [0][0].

PHP implementation details:
- Input: Use fscanf(STDIN, ...) or read lines.
- Output: echo or printf.
- Modulo: 1000000007.
- Matrix multiplication: Standard triple loop. Need to handle large numbers with modulo. PHP integers are arbitrary precision (since PHP 7/8), so no overflow issues like Go's int64, but modulo is required.
- Recursive power function: PHP has recursion limits, but usually okay for log2(n) depth. n can be up to 10^18? Go uses int64. PHP handles large ints fine.
- Matrix representation: Array of arrays.

Let's write the PHP code.

Structure:
<?php
// Fast I/O
$stdin = fopen('php://stdin', 'r');
$stdout = fopen('php://stdout', 'w');

function scanf($format, &...$vars) {
    global $stdin;
    return fscanf($stdin, $format, ...$vars);
}
function printf($format, ...$args) {
    global $stdout;
    return fprintf($stdout, $format, ...$args);
}

$mod = 1000000007;

// Read n, m
// Go code: scanf("%d %d\n", &n, &m)
$line = fgets($stdin);
sscanf($line, "%d %d", $n, $m);

if ($n < $m) {
    printf("1");
    exit;
}
if ($n == $m) {
    printf("2");
    exit;
}

// Initialize matrix
$matrix = array_fill(0, $m, array_fill(0, $m, 0));
$matrix[0][0] = 1;
$matrix[0][$m-1] = 1;
for ($i = 1; $i < $m; $i++) {
    $matrix[$i][$i-1] = 1;
}

function multiply($one, $two, $m, $mod) {
    $res = array_fill(0, $m, array_fill(0, $m, 0));
    for ($i = 0; $i < $m; $i++) {
        for ($j = 0; $j < $m; $j++) {
            $sum = 0;
            for ($k = 0; $k < $m; $k++) {
                $sum += ($one[$i][$k] * $two[$k][$j]) % $mod;
                // Keep sum manageable, though PHP handles big ints
                if ($sum > 1e18) $sum %= $mod; // Optimization
            }
            $res[$i][$j] = $sum % $mod;
        }
    }
    return $res;
}

function power($exp, $base, $m, $mod) {
    if ($exp == 1) {
        return $base;
    }
    $res = power($exp >> 1, $base, $m, $mod); // exp/2
    $res = multiply($res, $res, $m, $mod);
    if ($exp & 1) {
        $res = multiply($res, $base, $m, $mod);
    }
    return $res;
}

$resultMatrix = power($n, $matrix, $m, $mod);
printf("%d", $resultMatrix[0][0]);

fclose($stdin);
fclose($stdout);
?>

